  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Why is it called a hat-trick?",
      "scene": {
        "type": "statement",
        "hook": true,
        "text": "HAT|TRICK",
        "hi": 0
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "It didn't start in football. It started in cricket.",
      "scene": {
        "type": "statement",
        "text": "NOT FOOTBALL.|CRICKET.",
        "hi": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Sheffield, 1858. A bowler called H. H. Stephenson took three wickets with three balls.",
      "scene": {
        "type": "picture",
        "image": "hattrick1858",
        "label": "SHEFFIELD · 1858"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Three in three. Almost unheard of.",
      "scene": {
        "type": "counter",
        "from": 0,
        "to": 3,
        "word": "WICKETS · 3 BALLS",
        "size": 420
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "So, the story goes, the crowd had a whip-round, and bought him a hat.",
      "scene": {
        "type": "statement",
        "text": "A WHIP-ROUND|= A NEW HAT",
        "hi": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Three of something special became a hat-trick. And football borrowed it.",
      "scene": {
        "type": "statement",
        "text": "3 IN A ROW|= HAT-TRICK",
        "hi": 1
      }
    }
  ],
